  "query_preview": "..This morning's MCV has now moved to far southwest\nArizona and northwest Sonora with its outer cloud bands with\nvery weak showers over central and western Pima County. East of\nTucson isolated storms have formed over the White and\nChiricahua Mountains. Expect this activity to continue with \nstorm development over the higher terrain during the next couple\nof hours. In the valleys mesoanalysis still shows some \nconvective inhibition, but this will erode away with surface \nheating over the next few hours. Even with yesterday's activity \nenough instability and moisture will be in place for isolated to\nscattered thunderstorms this afternoon and evening mainly east \nof Tucson. The severe threat is less than the past few days, but\ncould still see some isolated severe wind gusts. The bigger \nthreat will be locally heavy rainfall leading to flash flooding \nespecially in areas that have received rain over the past few \ndays. Area washes and rivers such as the San Pedro are also \ncurrently running and will need to be monitored as more rain \nfalls. This afternoon the HREF paints Cochise County as having \nthe best chances for heavy rainfall. High temperatures will be a\nfew degrees above normal.\n\nFriday expect another round of afternoon and evening\nthunderstorms with storm chances expanding further west into\nTucson and central Pima County. The HREF is also showing higher\nprobabilities of strong to severe wind gusts with a large 10 \npercent contour for 50 kt winds or greater from Tucson eastward\ntomorrow afternoon and evening. Locally heavy rainfall will \nalso be a threat leading to flash flooding and rises in area \nwashes and rivers. \n\nThis weekend the ridge increases in strength, but will still be\nin a favorable area for daily afternoon and evening\nthunderstorms. NBM probabilities do show a downturn in storm\nchances along the AZ/NM border both Saturday and Sunday with the\nbest chances for storms extending from Ajo to highway 191.\nExpect normal monsoon hazards of isolated severe wind gusts and\nlocally heavy rainfall. Area washes and rivers will need to be\nmonitored for rises especially in watersheds that have seen\nrainfall each day this week. High temperatures increase a few\ndegrees becoming 5-8 degrees above normal Friday into Sunday.\nThis will lead to some areas of Major HeatRisk for the Tucson\nmetro and the Gila River Valley.  \n\nNext week heights decrease which results in temperatures falling\nto around normal Monday through at least mid-week. The center\nof the high sticks around the Four Corners region with\nthunderstorm chances expanding in coverage to cover most of\nsoutheast Arizona. Expect continued daily afternoon and evening\nthunderstorms next week.",
